          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many coats of sealer does marble need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "As many as it will absorb, and no more. Dense polished marble often takes one coat, while honed or porous marble usually takes two on the first treatment. The water test tells you: if water still darkens the stone after the cure, add another coat to those areas. Coats beyond that just dry on top."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I seal marble myself?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Sealing marble with an impregnating sealer is a straightforward DIY job if you clean thoroughly, let the stone dry, and wipe off every trace of excess. The jobs worth handing to a professional are stain removal on valuable stone, etch repair across large areas, and any honing or repolishing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does sealing marble change how it looks?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A non-enhancing impregnating sealer should not change the color or finish once it has cured, though solvent products can darken the stone slightly for a day or two while the carrier evaporates. Enhancing sealers are designed to darken and enrich the color permanently, so only use one if that is the effect you want."
