                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sealer type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Watch out for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Solvent-based impregnator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Dense polished marble, kitchens, longest life</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Strong odor, needs ventilation</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based impregnator</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Bathrooms, occupied homes, easy cleanup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">May need a second coat on polished stone</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Color enhancer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Honed, tumbled, textured marble</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Permanent darkening, test first</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Topical film coating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not recommended on marble counters</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Scratches, peels, traps moisture</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Step 1: Clean, Strip and Dry
          </h2>

          <p>
            Sealer cannot get past dirt, grease or old cleaner residue, and whatever is on the stone when you seal is locked in. Start by clearing the counter completely and washing it with a pH-neutral stone cleaner or warm water with a few drops of mild dish soap. Rinse with clean water so no soap film is left behind. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-clean-marble-countertops/">cleaning marble countertops</a> covers which products are safe and which quietly strip sealer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kitchen counters carry an invisible layer of cooking oil that a normal wipe does not remove. Many stone care guides, and the application section of our pillar, suggest a final wipe with acetone on a clean white cloth to cut that grease. Acetone does not etch marble, evaporates quickly and leaves nothing behind. Work with a window open and keep it well away from flames.
          </p>

          <p>
            Deal with stains before you seal, never after. Sealing over a stain locks it in, because the sealer lines the pores around the discoloration and makes it harder for any later treatment to reach. If there is a dark oil shadow or an organic mark, draw it out first with a poultice, following our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-stains-from-marble/">removing stains from marble</a>, and give the area a day or two to dry fully afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then wait. Water trapped in the stone blocks the sealer from getting in, so the marble must be completely dry. The usual guidance is at least 24 hours after any wet cleaning, longer after a poultice or if the room is cool and humid. A stone that still looks slightly darker in patches is still holding water. Sealing a damp counter is one of the commonest reasons a fresh job seems to fail within weeks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="apply" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Steps 2-4: Flood, Dwell, Wipe
          </h2>

          <p>
            Read the label on your specific product before you start, because dwell times and coat counts vary, and the manufacturer's instructions override any general advice. Then work in manageable sections, roughly two to four square feet at a time on a countertop, so that you can wipe each area off before it starts to dry. On a floor, work from the far side of the room towards the door.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flood the surface. Pour or spray the sealer on and spread it with a clean lint-free cloth, a foam brush or a lambswool applicator so the whole area is visibly wet. This is not paint; you want a wet, even layer, not a thin skim. Watch for spots that go dull quickly. Those are the thirstiest parts of the stone and need more sealer added while the rest is still wet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let it dwell. Most impregnating sealers specify somewhere around five to fifteen minutes of contact time, during which the stone draws the product into its pores. Keep the surface wet throughout by adding a little more where it dries out. Do not let any area dry on its own during the dwell period, because dried sealer on top of the stone is exactly what you are trying to avoid.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wipe it off completely. Before the dwell period ends and the product starts to tack up, wipe the whole section with clean, dry microfiber cloths, turning and replacing them as they load up. The goal is a surface that feels completely dry to the touch, with no slick or tacky areas. Buff once more with a fresh cloth. This step is where most home sealing jobs go wrong, and it is worth being almost obsessive about.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the label calls for a second coat, or the water test showed very fast absorption, wait the interval the manufacturer gives, often thirty minutes to an hour, and repeat the flood, dwell and wipe. A second coat goes on the same way and usually soaks in less, which is a good sign. Stop when the stone stops absorbing. Extra coats beyond that point simply sit on the surface.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Step 5: Cure, Then Test Again
          </h2>

          <p>
            An impregnating sealer feels dry within minutes of wiping, but the polymers inside the stone keep curing for a day or more. Keep water, cleaning products and anything heavy off the surface for at least 24 hours, or longer if the label says so. Our pillar guide suggests waiting around 72 hours before judging how well the stone repels stains, since full performance takes time to develop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solvent sealers can leave the stone looking slightly darker for a day or two while the carrier evaporates. That is normal and fades. If a non-enhancing sealer still has the marble noticeably darker after a week, something is wrong, usually product left on the surface or applied to damp stone, and the troubleshooting section below covers what to do about it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once cured, repeat the water test in the same spots you tested before. Water should now bead or at least sit on the surface without darkening the stone for fifteen minutes or more. If one area still darkens, it usually needed more product, so apply another coat to that section only and wipe it off as carefully as before. Do not reseal the whole counter to fix one thirsty spot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Write down the date and the product you used, and set a reminder to retest in six months. That is the honest way to decide when to reseal, rather than following a fixed calendar, and it is covered in our guide to <a href="/how-often-to-seal-marble/">how often to seal marble</a>. A solvent impregnator on a quiet vanity can last years, while the same product beside a busy kitchen sink may need topping up much sooner.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fixes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When It Goes Wrong: Haze, Dark Spots and Patchy Beading
          </h2>

          <p>
            Haze or streaks after sealing almost always mean sealer dried on the surface. The simplest fix, recommended by many manufacturers and on our pillar page, is to rewet the area with fresh sealer, which redissolves the dried residue, and then wipe it off hard with clean cloths before it dries again. If that does not shift it, check the product label for the residue remover the maker recommends rather than reaching for an abrasive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dark patches that do not fade after several days usually mean the stone was damp when it was sealed, or that an enhancing product was used by mistake. Moisture trapped under fresh sealer often works its way out over a week or two in a warm, dry room. If an enhancer has darkened a polished countertop unevenly, that is a job for a stone professional, because enhancement is designed to be permanent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Patchy beading, where water beads in some places but soaks in elsewhere, points to uneven application or uneven stone. Marble has veins and areas of different porosity, and the open areas need more product. Retest after the cure, mark the spots that still absorb, and give just those a further coat. Over the following months, the area around the sink will often be the first to lose protection, which is normal wear rather than failure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, dull spots that appear after sealing, especially near the sink or cooktop, are usually etches rather than sealer problems. Sealing does not protect marble against acids, so lemon juice, vinegar or tomato sauce will still dull the surface. That damage is repaired by polishing, not resealing, as covered in our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-etching-from-marble/">removing etching from marble</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing stone that already repels water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If the water test leaves no mark, the existing protection is working. Extra sealer cannot get in and simply dries on top, leaving haze and sticky patches that then have to be removed. Test first, and only seal the areas that actually absorb.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving sealer to dry on the surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The flood and dwell steps get attention, but the wipe-off decides how the job looks. Any product left on the stone dries into a cloudy film. Wipe each section completely dry with clean microfiber before it tacks up, then buff again.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over stains or damp stone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A stain sealed in is far harder to remove later, and water in the pores blocks the sealer. Poultice stains out first and give the marble at least a full day to dry after any wet cleaning or poultice before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a topical coating on countertops</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Film-forming sealers look good for a few weeks and then scratch, wear through around the sink and peel. Marble countertops need an impregnating sealer that works inside the stone and leaves the surface untouched.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting sealer to stop etching</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealing slows staining but does nothing against acids, which dissolve the surface itself. Dull rings from citrus, wine or vinegar are etches, not sealer failure, and they are fixed by polishing rather than by adding more sealer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does it take to seal marble?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The application itself takes twenty to forty minutes for a typical kitchen counter, including the dwell and wipe-off. Allow a day beforehand for the stone to dry after cleaning, and at least 24 hours afterwards before using the surface normally. Full stain resistance can take around 72 hours to develop, depending on the product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of sealer does marble need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                As many as it will absorb, and no more. Dense polished marble often takes one coat, while honed or porous marble usually takes two on the first treatment. The water test tells you: if water still darkens the stone after the cure, add another coat to those areas. Coats beyond that just dry on top.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal marble myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Sealing marble with an impregnating sealer is a straightforward DIY job if you clean thoroughly, let the stone dry, and wipe off every trace of excess. The jobs worth handing to a professional are stain removal on valuable stone, etch repair across large areas, and any honing or repolishing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does sealing marble change how it looks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A non-enhancing impregnating sealer should not change the color or finish once it has cured, though solvent products can darken the stone slightly for a day or two while the carrier evaporates. Enhancing sealers are designed to darken and enrich the color permanently, so only use one if that is the effect you want.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I seal marble tile and grout at the same time?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, and it is efficient on floors and shower walls. Most impregnating stone sealers also work on cement grout, and grout is often the more absorbent of the two. Check that the product is rated for both, and look at our grout sealer guide if the grout needs a dedicated product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is sealed marble food safe?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Once fully cured, most impregnating stone sealers sold for kitchen countertops are described by their makers as safe for food-contact surfaces, because the active ingredient sits inside the stone rather than on the surface. Check the specific product's label or data sheet, and keep the surface clear until the cure time has passed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if I never seal marble?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Unsealed marble absorbs oil, wine, coffee and colored foods quickly, so stains become more likely and harder to remove. It will not crack or deteriorate from a lack of sealer. Some very dense polished marbles absorb so slowly they rarely need sealing at all, which is why testing matters more than routine.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

